          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse and dense retrieval methods, particularly hybrid retrieval patterns that merge lexical and semantic search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single primary source on hybrid retrieval; reciprocal rank fusion confirmed across sources.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Performance and Use Cases\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nThis combination addresses the complementary strengths of both approaches: lexical search excels at exact matches and rare terms, while dense vector search captures semantic similarity and paraphrasing.\\n\\n### Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its practical effectiveness in balancing precision and recall across diverse query types.\\n\\n### Related Concepts\\n\\nUse reciprocal rank fusion as a merging strategy for combining ranked results from multiple retrieval methods.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval best practices in production RAG systems (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-04)\\n\u0022}"
